kosha combustion

  -- fuel + oxygen → carbon dioxide + water + heat.
  -- the most common exothermic reaction in everyday life.
  -- complete combustion: CxHy + O2 → CO2 + H2O (all carbon fully oxidised)
  -- incomplete combustion: insufficient O2 → CO (carbon monoxide) + soot (C).
  --
  -- methane:  CH4 + 2O2 → CO2 + 2H2O       ΔH = -890 kJ/mol
  -- ethanol:  C2H5OH + 3O2 → 2CO2 + 3H2O   ΔH = -1367 kJ/mol
  -- glucose:  C6H12O6 + 6O2 → 6CO2 + 6H2O  ΔH = -2803 kJ/mol
  -- octane:   2C8H18 + 25O2 → 16CO2 + 18H2O
  --
  -- activation energy: combustion needs ignition (spark, flame).
  -- once started, exothermic: the heat produced ignites more fuel → chain reaction.
  --
  -- biological relevance: cellular respiration is slow combustion.
  -- C6H12O6 + 6O2 → 6CO2 + 6H2O + ATP (same equation, different path, no flame).
  -- the energy is captured as ATP rather than released as heat.
  --
  -- combustion IS oxidation of carbon from C to C⁴⁺ (in CO2).
  -- the carbon oxidation state goes from 0 (in alkane) to +4 (in CO2).

